<!-- Three highlight questions on the key sentence. The verb is a single word;
     the subject and the object are graded as WHOLE PHRASES — every word of the
     phrase and nothing outside it.

     Grading the phrase as a phrase is the lesson. The next screen asks students
     to hold a noun phrase together as one unit; accepting the head word by
     itself here would teach the opposite on the screen before.

     The phrase that next screen takes apart is the OBJECT, not the subject:
     the subject here is the bare word "women," and the rich noun phrase is what
     the women took.

     The verb prompt names the two-verb structure, so what is scored is
     identifying a verb rather than guessing which of two coordinated verbs the
     author meant.

     Order is VERB → SUBJECT → OBJECT, not S-V-O. The verb is the one part you
     can find without having identified anything else; once you have it, "who
     did it" and "what got done to" are questions about the verb. Asking for the
     subject first asks students to find it out of nowhere. -->
<Vertical id="tmb_grammar_svo">
<Markdown id="tmb_grammar_svo_md">
## Verb, Subject, Object

Three quick highlights on the key sentence — the verb first, then the subject,
then the object.

The verb is a **single word**. The subject and the object are **whole phrases**:
highlight every word of the phrase, and nothing outside it.
</Markdown>

<CapaProblem id="tmb_grammar_svo_verb_problem" title="Highlight the verb" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmb_grammar_svo_verb_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmb_grammar_svo_verb">
This sentence has two verbs joined by "and." Highlight the first one — a single word.
---
Yet women [took] the very ideology that defined their place in the home and used it to fashion a public role for themselves.
---
all: Right — "took" is the first verb: what women did with that ideology before they used it.
: The verb is the action word — here "took," the first thing women did in the sentence.
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_grammar_svo_verb_problem -->

<CapaProblem id="tmb_grammar_svo_subject_problem" title="Highlight the subject" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmb_grammar_svo_subject_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmb_grammar_svo_subject">
Highlight the subject — the entire subject phrase, every word of it.
---
Yet [women] took the very ideology that defined their place in the home and used it to fashion a public role for themselves.
---
all: Right — the subject is "women." Here the whole subject phrase is that one word: they are the ones who did the taking.
: The subject is who or what does the action — "women," the ones who did the taking.
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_grammar_svo_subject_problem -->

<CapaProblem id="tmb_grammar_svo_object_problem" title="Highlight the object" maxAttempts="1">
<TextSelectionGrader id="tmb_grammar_svo_object_grader">
<TextSelectionInput id="tmb_grammar_svo_object">
Highlight the direct object of that first verb — the entire object phrase, every word of it.
---
Yet women took [the very ideology that defined their place in the home] and used it to fashion a public role for themselves.
---
all: Right — the object is the whole phrase "the very ideology that defined their place in the home." Its head noun is "ideology"; the rest of the phrase tells you which ideology.
: The object is what women took — the whole phrase "the very ideology that defined their place in the home," built around the noun "ideology."
</TextSelectionInput>
</TextSelectionGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_grammar_svo_object_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_grammar_svo -->

<!-- Inside the OBJECT noun phrase. ONE matching item, where three sequential
     highlights used to be. The parts on the right are the constituents of the
     phrase the student has just finished analyzing as a whole; naming them is
     recognition, and recognition of three related labels is one question, not
     three. Three separate one-attempt highlights would also mean three Check
     buttons and three chances to be told nothing useful.

     Matching also carries partial credit, so a student who has the head noun
     and the postmodifier but not the determiner is scored as such.

     The phrase is shown WITHOUT the rest of the sentence: the rest of the
     sentence is nothing but a place to make mistakes here.

     "Determiner," not "premodifier" — it is the category the course uses for
     the pointing word(s) in front of the head noun. "the very" points at one
     particular ideology, so it does the same job "Such" does in the parallel
     form. -->
<Vertical id="tmb_grammar_np">
<Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_md">
## Inside the Noun Phrase

The object of that sentence is a noun phrase all by itself. Here it is on its
own — take it apart.
</Markdown>

<CapaProblem id="tmb_grammar_np_problem" title="Inside the Noun Phrase" maxAttempts="1">
<MatchingGrader id="tmb_grammar_np_grader">
<Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_prompt">
Match each part of a noun phrase to the part that plays it in **the very
ideology that defined their place in the home**.
</Markdown>
<MatchingInput id="tmb_grammar_np_match">
  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_role_head"> **Head noun** — the one word the whole phrase is built around                     </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_part_head"> ideology                                                                         </Markdown>

  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_role_det" > **Determiner** — the word before the head noun that helps identify or specify it </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_part_det" > the                                                                              </Markdown>

  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_role_post"> **Postmodifier** — the part after the head noun that tells you more about it     </Markdown>
  <Markdown id="tmb_grammar_np_part_post"> that defined their place in the home                                             </Markdown>
</MatchingInput>
</MatchingGrader>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_grammar_np_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_grammar_np -->

<!-- The structure, laid out (info screen) -->
<Vertical id="tmb_grammar_structure">
<Markdown id="tmb_grammar_structure_md">
## The Grammatical Structure

Here is the sentence laid out:

> **[ women ]** — subject (a noun)
> **took** — verb
> **[ the very ideology that defined their place in the home ]** — direct object (a noun phrase)
> *and used it to fashion a public role for themselves* — a second verb phrase, joined to the first by "and"

The object is a **noun phrase** — to understand the sentence, you hold the
whole phrase together.
</Markdown>
<Done id="tmb_grammar_structure_done" label="I understand" />
</Vertical>

<!-- Highlight the sentence that first NAMES the ideology "the very ideology"
     points back to. Only one sentence in the passage names it ("cult of
     domesticity" / "cult of true womanhood"), so the target is unambiguous
     even though the passage spans several paragraphs. -->
<SimpleTextSelection id="tmb_grammar_antecedent" mode="graded" title="Find the Antecedent">
"The very ideology that defined their place in the home" points back to an ideology the text has already named. Find the earlier sentence that first gives it that name — the one that calls it the "cult of domesticity" — and highlight the entire sentence, from its first word through its final period.
---
[Femininity in the nation was intimately tied to the domestic sphere in what historians have described as the "cult of domesticity," or the "cult of true womanhood."] These ideas developed in tandem with industrialization, the market revolution, and the Second Great Awakening. In reality, they applied primarily to upper-class and middle-upper-class women. In the era of revivalism and reform, Americans understood the family and home as the hearthstones of civic virtue and moral influence. Women were expected to be the guardians of virtue and the spiritual heads of the home. They were to be pious, pure, submissive, and domestic. Yet women took the very ideology that defined their place in the home and used it to fashion a public role for themselves.
---
all: Yes — that earlier sentence is where the text names the ideology: the "cult of domesticity," or "cult of true womanhood." That is the ideology "the very ideology" points back to.
: "The very ideology" points to an ideology the text has already named — the sentence that first calls it the "cult of domesticity."
</SimpleTextSelection>


