<Vertical id="tmb_article_read">
<Markdown id="tmb_article_md">
## The Magazine Article — On Paper

Your instructor handed out a printed copy of the magazine article about the
"tradwife" trend. **Read it on paper now.**

Read it the same way you read the textbook on screen — with a pen or a
highlighter in hand:

- **Mark the three words** the article's discussion asks you to watch for:
  *tradwife*, *economic precarity*, and *egalitarian.*
- **Underline** anything that says what the "tradwife" trend really looks like
  for most stay-at-home mothers, and why.
- **Star** anything that reminds you of the *American Yawp* excerpt or the
  engraving — you'll need those connections shortly.

Keep the handout beside you. The questions in the conversation on the left are
all about it, and you'll draw on it again when you pull the three texts
together at the end.

*If you don't have a copy, ask your instructor before going on.*
</Markdown>
<!-- The reading happens off-screen, so the chat has nothing to watch except
       this: the student tells us when the paper article has been read. -->
<Done id="tmb_article_read_done" label="I've read the handout" />
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_article_read -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 4 — The engraving (third text): N. unknown, "The Sphere of
     Woman," Godey's Lady's Book vol. 40 (March 1850) — Figure 11.3 in
     the American Yawp chapter. Public domain.
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_img_read">
<Markdown id="tmb_img_intro">
## A Period Image: "The Sphere of Woman"

Study this engraving from an 1850 women's magazine. Notice **where the woman
is** (indoors, at home), **what she is doing** (caring for her children), and
the **title** printed beneath the picture.
</Markdown>
<Image id="tmb_img_img" src="images/sphere_of_woman.jpg" width="640"
  alt="'The Sphere of Woman' (Godey's Lady's Book, 1850): inside a curtained parlor, a mother holds her baby while a girl and a boy read and play beside her, a dog resting at their feet; the title 'THE SPHERE OF WOMAN' is printed beneath the scene."
  authors="Godey's Lady's Book, 1850" license="Public domain" />
<Markdown id="tmb_img_caption">
**"The Sphere of Woman"** — *Godey's Lady's Book,* vol. 40 (March 1850).

A mother holds her baby while two children read and play beside her in a
comfortable parlor. *Godey's Lady's Book* was the most popular women's magazine
of the era, and it printed this engraving as a picture of a woman's proper
place: the home, at the center of family and children.
</Markdown>
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_img_read -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 4 — Synthesis: sort each description as "then" (the 1800s
     ideal) vs "now" (the tradwife trend), drawing on all three texts. Graded:
     one attempt, key revealed after submission, not before (see the
     note-check grid).
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_synth_sort">
<Markdown id="tmb_synth_sort_prompt">
## Then and Now

Using the textbook, the printed article, and the engraving, sort each description
under the movement it best fits. Then press **Check.**
</Markdown>
<CapaProblem id="tmb_synth_sort_problem" title="Then vs. Now" maxAttempts="1">
<TabularMCQGrader id="tmb_synth_sort_grader">
<TabularMCQ id="tmb_synth_sort_grid">
cols:
  - "The 1800s ideal of the 'true woman'"
  - "Today's 'tradwife' trend"
rows:
  - "Held up the pious, pure, submissive woman at home as a moral and religious duty|s_ideal[The 1800s ideal of the 'true woman']"
  - "Justified by the belief that women were the natural guardians of virtue and the home|s_guardian[The 1800s ideal of the 'true woman']"
  - "Applied mainly to upper- and middle-class women, while working-class women worked out of necessity|s_class[The 1800s ideal of the 'true woman']"
  - "Spread through social-media images of glamorous, calm stay-at-home mothers|s_social[Today's 'tradwife' trend]"
  - "Often driven less by free choice than by economic precarity — costly child care and unequal pay|s_precarity[Today's 'tradwife' trend]"
</TabularMCQ>
</TabularMCQGrader>
</CapaProblem>
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_synth_sort -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 5 — Culminating writing task (Pulling It Together).
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_final_write_screen">
<Markdown id="tmb_final_write_prompt">
## Pulling It Together

This is the goal the whole module has been building toward.

**In four or five sentences, explain the 1800s ideal of the "true woman" (the
"cult of domesticity") and today's "tradwife" trend. Then compare and contrast
them: in what ways are the two alike, and how do they differ?**

Draw on all three texts — the *American Yawp* excerpt, the engraving, and the
article your instructor handed out.
</Markdown>
<TextArea id="tmb_final_write" rows="8"
  placeholder="Both hold up the woman at home as an ideal, but…" />

<!-- Progress affordance, two tiers. There is no word minimum to hit: the
       conversation moves on as soon as the student has really begun, so tier 1
       is encouragement rather than a countdown. The second tier only ever
       claims what it actually checked (length); quality is handed back to the
       student as a read-it-back checklist. -->
<Markdown when="wordcount(@tmb_final_write.value) &lt; 40">
*A solid comparison usually takes four or five sentences — keep going.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="wordcount(@tmb_final_write.value) &gt;= 40">
*✓ You can continue. Before you do, read it back: does it name **one way the two are alike** and **one way they differ**? Does it draw on **all three texts** — the textbook, the engraving, and the article?*
</Markdown>
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_final_write_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     Part 5 — Conclusion / wrap-up.
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_conclusion">
<Markdown id="tmb_conclusion_md">
## You're Done

Nice work — you've finished the module. Along the way you practiced the full
range of READ 1000 reading skills on **three real texts:**

- **using context for vocabulary** (domesticity, ideology, en masse, euphemism, and more)
- **close, academic-language reading** (the subject–verb–object of a hard sentence, and its antecedent)
- **reading comprehension** across the textbook and the article
- **organizing notes** under headings
- **analyzing an image** as persuasion, and finally
- **synthesizing** all three texts into four or five sentences comparing the 1800s ideal of womanhood with today's "tradwife" trend.

Here is the finished work — the comparison you built from all three texts.
</Markdown>
<!-- The writing screen is behind us in the pane by now, so the conclusion
       shows the comparison rather than pointing at a screen that has moved
       on. <Use>, not a copy: same block, same state, still editable. -->
<Use ref="tmb_final_write" />
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_conclusion -->


