<!-- Each chat-embedded question is wrapped in a Vertical that TAKES OVER the
     embed id (`::tmb_q_publicsphere_screen`), so the correctness feedback under
     the choices renders inside the chat bubble with them. The ChoiceInput
     keeps its own id — every wait, and every readback, still names it.

     Inside that Vertical the question is a one-attempt CapaProblem
     (`<id>_problem`, maxAttempts="1") around a KeyGrader around the
     ChoiceInput: this is an assessment, and each item is answered once. The
     Check button is the CapaProblem's own footer. The ChoiceInput id and every
     option value are unchanged — the chatpeg gates and the analytics read
     them. maxAttempts is spelled out because "0" means unlimited, not none.

     Every per-choice verdict below is gated on `<id>_problem.submitCount` as
     well as on the chosen value. Keyed on the value alone, a student with a
     Check button in front of them could click each option in turn and read
     which one comes back "Yes" before ever submitting — the whole item,
     answered for them, under a rule that allows one attempt.

     Register: each line is the explanation that follows a submission, not a
     hint toward a second try — there is none. A wrong-choice line says why
     that option misses and what the key is; nothing tells the student to
     look again. -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_publicsphere_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_publicsphere_problem" title="Vocabulary: public sphere" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_publicsphere_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_publicsphere">
<Markdown>
Look at the highlighted sentence and choose the best definition for **public sphere**:

> "In the **public sphere**, women increasingly challenged the moral direction
> of this new American society."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="private_home"  > The private world of the household and family.         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="town_square"   > A large public square or park in the center of a town. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="shared_opinion"> A group of people who all share the same opinion.      </Distractor>
         <Key value="public_life"   > Life outside the home — work, politics, and community. </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_publicsphere.value === 'private_home' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_publicsphere_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the **domestic** sphere, the opposite of the public one. The public sphere is life outside the home — work, politics, community.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_publicsphere.value === 'town_square' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_publicsphere_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"Sphere" here isn't a literal place you can stand in. It means a whole area of life — work, politics, community — outside the home.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_publicsphere.value === 'shared_opinion' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_publicsphere_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The public sphere is where people act and argue in common, not a single shared opinion: the shared world outside the home.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_publicsphere.value === 'public_life' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_publicsphere_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the public sphere is life outside the home: work, politics, and community. Its opposite is the "domestic sphere," which comes up next.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_publicsphere_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_publicsphere_screen -->

<!-- CHAT-EMBEDDED — vocabulary, chunk A2 / para 2 -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_tandem_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_tandem_problem" title="Vocabulary: tandem" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_tandem_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_tandem">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **in tandem with** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "These ideas developed **in tandem with** industrialization, the market
> revolution, and the Second Great Awakening."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="before"   > long before; ahead of             </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="against"  > in opposition to; working against </Distractor>
         <Key value="alongside"> at the same time as; alongside    </Key>
  <Distractor value="instead"  > instead of; in place of           </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tandem.value === 'against' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tandem_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Backwards. The ideas didn't fight those changes; they grew up right alongside them — "in tandem with" means together, at the same time.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tandem.value === 'before' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tandem_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"In tandem" is about the same time, not one ahead of the other: two riders on a tandem bicycle move together.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tandem.value === 'instead' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tandem_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The ideas didn't replace industrialization or the revival; they developed together with them, at the same time.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tandem.value === 'alongside' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tandem_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — "in tandem with" means together, at the same time. A tandem bicycle carries two riders moving as one.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_tandem_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_tandem_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — cult of domesticity, disciplinary term (chunk A2)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_cd_term_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_cd_term_problem" title="Cult of domesticity: the term" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_cd_term_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_cd_term">
<Markdown>
**Cult of domesticity** is a discipline-specific term. Using clues in the
passage, which phrase most directly helps us understand what it was?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="market_revolution"  > the market revolution        </Distractor>
         <Key value="domestic_sphere"    > the domestic sphere          </Key>
  <Distractor value="public_sphere"      > the public sphere            </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="second_awakening"   > the Second Great Awakening   </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_term.value === 'market_revolution' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The market revolution is one of the changes the idea developed alongside, not what the idea WAS. The passage's own phrase for it is "the domestic sphere."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_term.value === 'public_sphere' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the opposite. The cult of domesticity tied women to the HOME — "the domestic sphere," in the passage's words — away from public life.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_term.value === 'second_awakening' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The Second Great Awakening helped shape the idea, but it isn't a definition of it. The passage's phrase for what it was is "the domestic sphere."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_term.value === 'domestic_sphere' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_term_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the passage hands you the phrase: femininity was "intimately tied to the domestic sphere." A woman's proper place was the home.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_cd_term_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_cd_term_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_cd_connect_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_cd_connect_problem" title="Cult of domesticity: what connects to it" maxAttempts="1">
<CheckboxGrader id="tmb_cd_connect_grader">
<CheckboxInput id="tmb_cd_connect">
<Markdown>
The cult of domesticity was the idea that a woman's proper place was the
**home.** What other words or phrases from the chapter describe or connect to
it? **Select all that apply.**
</Markdown>
         <Key value="true_womanhood"       > "cult of true womanhood"           </Key>
         <Key value="guardians_of_virtue"   > guardians of virtue                </Key>
  <Distractor value="reform_societies"      > reform societies                   </Distractor>
         <Key value="pious_pure_submissive" > pious, pure, submissive, domestic  </Key>
  <Distractor value="antislavery_movement"  > the antislavery movement           </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="working_class_labor"   > working-class women's labor        </Distractor>
</CheckboxInput>
</CheckboxGrader>

<!-- Multi-select, so the feedback has to cover three cases: a wrong pick, a
   right-but-incomplete set, and the whole set.

   SCORING RULE: exact match. CheckboxGrader with no partialCredit marks the
   response correct only when all three Keys and no Distractor are selected;
   anything else is simply wrong. That is a strict rule for a six-option
   select-all, and it is declared here as the rule this form scores by, not
   as the only defensible one — partial credit is the obvious next question. -->
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('reform_societies') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Reform societies are the PUBLIC role women fashioned once they left the home, not part of the domestic ideal.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('antislavery_movement') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Joining the antislavery movement took women OUT into public life — the opposite of staying in the domestic sphere.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('working_class_labor') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Working-class women joined the public sphere through their labor, out of necessity. They're the exception to the ideal, not part of it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_connect.value.length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect.value.length &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Fewer than the full set. Three phrases describe the "true woman" at home: the "cult of true womanhood," women as guardians of virtue, and the qualities she was to have — pious, pure, submissive, domestic.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_connect.value.length === 3 &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('true_womanhood') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('guardians_of_virtue') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect.value.includes('pious_pure_submissive') &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_connect_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the set: the "cult of true womanhood," women as guardians of virtue, and the qualities of the ideal woman — pious, pure, submissive, and domestic.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_cd_connect_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_cd_connect_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_cd_bestdef_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_cd_bestdef_problem" title="Cult of domesticity: the best definition" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_cd_bestdef_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_cd_bestdef">
<Markdown>
Each member of your group wrote a one-sentence description of the cult of
domesticity. Read them and choose the **best** one.
</Markdown>
         <Key value="joe_home_ideal"          > **Joe:** The belief that a true woman belonged at home — pious, pure, submissive, domestic — raising her children in virtue.      </Key>
  <Distractor value="danielle_reform_movement"> **Danielle:** The name for the movement that sent middle-class women out of their homes to join antislavery and reform societies. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="karis_laws_all_women"    > **Karis:** A set of laws passed in the 1830s requiring women, rich and poor, to stay home and manage their households.            </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_bestdef.value === 'danielle_reform_movement' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Danielle has it backwards. That's the PUBLIC role women fashioned FROM the ideology; the cult of domesticity itself was about staying home.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_bestdef.value === 'karis_laws_all_women' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Karis makes it a law for everyone. It was a cultural and religious ideal, not a law, and it applied mainly to upper- and middle-class women.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_cd_bestdef.value === 'joe_home_ideal' &amp;&amp; @tmb_cd_bestdef_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — Joe's has all the pieces: the home as a woman's proper place, the qualities of the "true woman," and passing virtue on to her children.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_cd_bestdef_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_cd_bestdef_screen -->


