<Vertical id="tmb_q_ideology_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_ideology_problem" title="Vocabulary: ideology" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_ideology_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_ideology">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **ideology**:

> "Yet women took the very **ideology** that defined their place in the home and
> used it to fashion a public role for themselves."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="single_feeling"> A single strong feeling or emotion.                     </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="government_law"> A law passed by the government.                         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="mind_study"    > The scientific study of how the mind and behavior work. </Distractor>
         <Key value="belief_system" > A set of shared beliefs about how society should work.  </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_ideology.value === 'single_feeling' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_ideology_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*An ideology is bigger than one feeling. It's a whole system of beliefs about how things should be — here, that a woman's place is the home.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_ideology.value === 'government_law' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_ideology_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Laws are written and enforced; an ideology is a set of shared beliefs. The cult of domesticity was an ideology, not a law.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_ideology.value === 'mind_study' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_ideology_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's "psychology." The "-ology" ending fools a lot of people; an ideology is a set of beliefs about how society should work.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_ideology.value === 'belief_system' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_ideology_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — a shared set of beliefs about how society should work. Here it's the belief that a woman's place is the home.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_ideology_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_ideology_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_enmasse_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_enmasse_problem" title="Vocabulary: en masse" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_enmasse_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_enmasse">
<Markdown>
What words could take the place of **en masse** — but keep the meaning the same?

> "…enabled white middle-class women, especially in the North, to leave their
> homes **en masse**…"
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="one_at_a_time"> one at a time, slowly         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="in_secret"    > in secret, without being seen </Distractor>
         <Key value="all_together" > in a large group, at once     </Key>
  <Distractor value="by_force"     > by force, against their will  </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_enmasse.value === 'one_at_a_time' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_enmasse_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The opposite. "En masse" means as a large group, all at once.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_enmasse.value === 'in_secret' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_enmasse_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Nothing about "en masse" is hidden. It's about numbers — a whole crowd, all together, out in the open.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_enmasse.value === 'by_force' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_enmasse_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*"En masse" is about how MANY, not whether they were forced. The women left in large numbers, of their own accord.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_enmasse.value === 'all_together' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_enmasse_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — "en masse" is French for "in a mass" — in large numbers, all together. Watch for it again in the article.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_enmasse_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_enmasse_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_moralizing_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_moralizing_problem" title="Vocabulary: moralizing" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_moralizing_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_moralizing">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **moralizing** as it's used here:

> "Southern women were more often instructed to offer a calming, **moralizing**
> influence on husbands and children…"
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="discouraging"    > Lowering someone's confidence; discouraging them. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="guilt_tripping"  > Making people feel guilty on purpose.             </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="cheering_up"     > Boosting a team's spirit before a game.           </Distractor>
         <Key value="teaching_right"  > Encouraging good behavior and right living.       </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_moralizing.value === 'discouraging' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_moralizing_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's "demoralizing," which is nearly the opposite. "Moralizing" is about morals — encouraging right living.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_moralizing.value === 'guilt_tripping' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_moralizing_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A moralizing influence encourages GOOD behavior; it isn't about guilt. The passage pairs it with "calming."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_moralizing.value === 'cheering_up' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_moralizing_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's "boosting morale," a different word. "Moralizing" is about morals — encouraging good behavior — not team spirit.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_moralizing.value === 'teaching_right' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_moralizing_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — a moralizing influence encourages good behavior and right living. It fits the idea of women as the "guardians of virtue."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_moralizing_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_moralizing_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_euphemism_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_euphemism_problem" title="Vocabulary: euphemism" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_euphemism_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_euphemism">
<Markdown>
Choose the best definition for **euphemism**:

> "Female virtue, particularly in the South, came to be understood largely as a
> **euphemism** for sexual purity."
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="opposite_meaning" > A word that means the exact opposite of what it says.    </Distractor>
         <Key value="polite_substitute"> A polite word used in place of a blunter one.            </Key>
  <Distractor value="foreign_word"     > A word borrowed from another language and kept as it is. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="short_lesson"     > A short, memorable saying that teaches a lesson.         </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_euphemism.value === 'opposite_meaning' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_euphemism_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's irony or sarcasm. A euphemism doesn't reverse the meaning; it softens it — a polite word in place of a blunter one.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_euphemism.value === 'foreign_word' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_euphemism_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A euphemism can be a perfectly ordinary English word. What makes it a euphemism is that it's a gentler stand-in for a blunter one.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_euphemism.value === 'short_lesson' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_euphemism_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's a proverb or an aphorism. A euphemism is a polite substitute for a more direct word.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_euphemism.value === 'polite_substitute' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_euphemism_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — a euphemism is a milder, more polite word standing in for a blunter one. Here, "female virtue" was a gentle way of saying "sexual purity."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_euphemism_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_euphemism_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — why did women turn the ideology into a public role?
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_why_publicrole_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_why_publicrole_problem" title="How women fashioned a public role" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_why_publicrole_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_why_publicrole">
<Markdown>
So how did women take "the very ideology that defined their place in the home"
and turn it into a **public** role?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="rejected_ideology"> They rejected the ideology and demanded the same jobs and wages as men.                       </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="factory_owners"   > Factory owners required middle-class women to leave home and take paid work in the new mills. </Distractor>
         <Key value="moral_authority"  > It cast women as guardians of virtue, and they used that moral authority to act in public.    </Key>
  <Distractor value="government_role"  > State governments passed laws giving women a formal role in public life.                      </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_why_publicrole.value === 'rejected_ideology' &amp;&amp; @tmb_why_publicrole_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Women "took the very ideology" and USED it. They didn't reject it; they turned the moral authority it gave them into a reason to act in public.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_why_publicrole.value === 'factory_owners' &amp;&amp; @tmb_why_publicrole_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That was working-class women, who labored out of necessity. Middle-class women's public role grew out of the moral authority the ideology gave them, not factory work.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_why_publicrole.value === 'government_role' &amp;&amp; @tmb_why_publicrole_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*No law gave them this role. They claimed it themselves, out of the moral authority the ideology already granted them.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_why_publicrole.value === 'moral_authority' &amp;&amp; @tmb_why_publicrole_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — if a woman is the guardian of virtue and morality, then cleaning up society becomes her job too. That's how the home-bound ideal became a reason to act in public.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_why_publicrole_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_why_publicrole_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — article vocabulary + comprehension
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_tradwife_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_tradwife_problem" title="Vocabulary: tradwife" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_tradwife_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_tradwife">
<Markdown>
The article is about **tradwives.** First — what does **tradwife** mean?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="trades_stocks"        > A woman who trades or invests money for a living.                     </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="working_mother"       > A mother who works full-time outside the home while raising children. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="marriage_activist"    > A woman who campaigns for traditional marriage laws.                  </Distractor>
         <Key value="traditional_homemaker"> A woman who embraces the traditional homemaker role.                  </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tradwife.value === 'trades_stocks' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tradwife_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A reasonable guess from "trad," but it's short for **traditional**, not "trade." A tradwife embraces a traditional wife-and-homemaker role.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tradwife.value === 'working_mother' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tradwife_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Nearly the opposite. The tradwife image is about staying HOME as a traditional homemaker, not working outside it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tradwife.value === 'marriage_activist' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tradwife_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*A tradwife is defined by the role she lives — traditional homemaker — not by campaigning for laws.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_tradwife.value === 'traditional_homemaker' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_tradwife_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — "trad" is short for traditional. A tradwife embraces the traditional stay-at-home wife-and-mother role.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_tradwife_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_tradwife_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_precarity">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_precarity_pick_problem" title="Economic precarity: the best sentence" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_precarity_pick_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_precarity_pick">
<Markdown>
The article's key idea is **economic precarity.** To pin down what it means, each
of us found a sentence from the article that gives it context. Who picked the
**best** one?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="danielle_political"      > **Danielle:** "Conservative commentators embraced them as evidence that women want to stay home."                                        </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="karis_businesswomen"     > **Karis:** "The women who post about their impeccable meals and beloved husbands might be better understood as businesswomen; some are making huge sums." </Distractor>
         <Key value="joe_low_income"          > **Joe:** "Many are just exhausted, low-income moms who can't afford child care."                                                      </Key>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_precarity_pick.value === 'danielle_political' &amp;&amp; @tmb_precarity_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Danielle's sentence is about the political fight over tradwives. Economic **precarity** needs a sentence about money and insecurity.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_precarity_pick.value === 'karis_businesswomen' &amp;&amp; @tmb_precarity_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Karis found the well-off influencers, who are the opposite of precarious. Precarity is about insecurity, not "making huge sums."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_precarity_pick.value === 'joe_low_income' &amp;&amp; @tmb_precarity_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — "exhausted, low-income moms who can't afford child care" shows exactly what economic precarity is: financial insecurity that leaves people few choices.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_precarity_pick_problem -->

<Markdown>**Explain why you chose that sentence.**</Markdown>
<TextArea id="tmb_precarity_explain" rows="3" placeholder="I chose that sentence because…" />
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_precarity -->

<Vertical id="tmb_summary_pick_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_summary_pick_problem" title="The best summary of the first paragraph" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_summary_pick_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_summary_pick">
<Markdown>
Which is the **best summary** of the article's first paragraph (about how
"tradwives" appeared)?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="image_only"  > Tradwives are women with glossy hair who post videos of themselves cooking meat from scratch and soothing calm babies in spotless farmhouse kitchens. </Distractor>
         <Key value="what_and_why"> A few years ago, images of glamorous "tradwives" spread on social media and became a political flashpoint: praise on the right, attacks on the left.  </Key>
  <Distractor value="overstated"  > Almost overnight, tradwives were everywhere online and became one of the biggest fights in American politics.                                         </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="opinion"     > Tradwives are one of the most fascinating internet trends of the last few years, and it's easy to see why so many people admire them.                 </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_summary_pick.value === 'image_only' &amp;&amp; @tmb_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*True, but only the picture. A summary has to carry the point: that the trend became a culture-war flashpoint over what women want.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_summary_pick.value === 'overstated' &amp;&amp; @tmb_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That overstates it — the paragraph says the trend became a flashpoint, not the biggest issue in politics. A summary shouldn't exaggerate what it reports.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_summary_pick.value === 'opinion' &amp;&amp; @tmb_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's an opinion about tradwives, not a summary of the paragraph. Summaries report; they don't rate.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_summary_pick.value === 'what_and_why' &amp;&amp; @tmb_summary_pick_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — what appeared (glamorous tradwife images), where (social media), and the point: both sides turned it into a fight over what women want.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_summary_pick_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_summary_pick_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_q_egalitarian_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_q_egalitarian_problem" title="Vocabulary: egalitarian" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_q_egalitarian_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_q_egalitarian">
<Markdown>
The article says many couples want "an **egalitarian** division of labor." What
does **egalitarian** mean?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="by_earnings"      > Decided by whoever earns the most money.            </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="traditional"      > Following traditional roles, with the wife at home. </Distractor>
         <Key value="based_on_equality"> Based on equality between the partners.             </Key>
  <Distractor value="both_must_work"   > Requiring both partners to work outside the home.   </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_q_egalitarian.value === 'by_earnings' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_egalitarian_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's what actually happens in the article — the role falls to whoever earns less — but it's the opposite of egalitarian, which means equal.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_egalitarian.value === 'traditional' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_egalitarian_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*That's the traditional arrangement. "Egalitarian" points the other way: toward equality between the partners.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_egalitarian.value === 'both_must_work' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_egalitarian_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Egalitarian is about sharing the work equally, not about forcing both partners into outside jobs.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_q_egalitarian.value === 'based_on_equality' &amp;&amp; @tmb_q_egalitarian_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Right — "egalitarian" comes from "equal." An egalitarian division of labor shares the work evenly between partners.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_q_egalitarian_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_q_egalitarian_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_article_thesis_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_article_thesis_problem" title="The article's main point" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_article_thesis_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_article_thesis">
<Markdown>
Step back from the details — what is the article's **main point** overall?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="mostly_wealthy"        > Most tradwives are wealthy influencers who make huge sums of money posting videos of their homes, meals, and children online.     </Distractor>
         <Key value="glossy_hides_precarity"> Behind the glossy "tradwife" image, many stay-at-home mothers are pushed there by economic precarity, not a dream of domesticity. </Key>
  <Distractor value="women_shouldnt_stay"   > Women today are better off working than staying home to raise their children, whatever they may prefer.                           </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="free_childcare"        > The government should make child care affordable so that more mothers can return to paid work.                                    </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_article_thesis.value === 'mostly_wealthy' &amp;&amp; @tmb_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The wealthy influencers are one detail. The article's point is about the MANY who are low-income: the glossy image hides economic precarity.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_article_thesis.value === 'women_shouldnt_stay' &amp;&amp; @tmb_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The article never says women shouldn't stay home. It says many aren't choosing it freely — they're pushed by money, which the glossy image hides.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_article_thesis.value === 'free_childcare' &amp;&amp; @tmb_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Expensive child care is part of the problem the article describes, but "make child care free" isn't the point it's arguing. The point is what the glossy image hides.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_article_thesis.value === 'glossy_hides_precarity' &amp;&amp; @tmb_article_thesis_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — the shiny image and the harder reality. Behind the glossy videos, most stay-at-home moms are there because of economic precarity, not a dream of domesticity.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_article_thesis_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_article_thesis_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — image comprehension (Part 4)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_img_observe_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_img_observe_problem" title="The engraving: what it shows" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_img_observe_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_img_observe">
<Markdown>
What does the engraving show?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="reform_meeting"> A group of women meeting to organize a reform society.              </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="factory_work"  > A woman working alongside men at the machines in a textile factory. </Distractor>
         <Key value="mother_at_home"> A mother at home with her baby and two children.                    </Key>
  <Distractor value="classroom"     > A teacher leading a classroom full of young students.               </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_img_observe.value === 'reform_meeting' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*There's no meeting here. The scene is a home: a mother with her baby and two children in a parlor.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_observe.value === 'factory_work' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*No factory, no men. Everything in the picture points to the home and the family — a mother with her children.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_observe.value === 'classroom' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*It isn't a classroom. It's a mother at home with her own children — the title even calls it "the sphere of woman."*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_observe.value === 'mother_at_home' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_observe_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — a peaceful domestic scene: a mother at home with her children. The caption names it "the sphere of woman."*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_img_observe_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_img_observe_screen -->

<Vertical id="tmb_img_purpose_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_img_purpose_problem" title="The engraving: why it was made" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_img_purpose_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_img_purpose">
<Markdown>
Why would a popular women's magazine publish an image like this, titled "The
Sphere of Woman"?
</Markdown>
         <Key value="promote_ideal"     > To hold up the home as a woman's proper place.               </Key>
  <Distractor value="record_real_family"> To record what one specific real family looked like in 1850. </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="advertise_toys"    > To advertise the toys and furniture shown in the scene.      </Distractor>
  <Distractor value="encourage_public"  > To encourage women to leave the home for public life.        </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_img_purpose.value === 'record_real_family' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*It isn't a snapshot of a real family. It's an ideal — a picture of how a "true woman's" life was supposed to look, published to promote it.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_purpose.value === 'advertise_toys' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The objects aren't the point. The scene is selling an idea: that a woman belongs at home with her children.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_purpose.value === 'encourage_public' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*The opposite. The whole image keeps the woman inside the home, at the center of the family — that's the ideal it promotes.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_img_purpose.value === 'promote_ideal' &amp;&amp; @tmb_img_purpose_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — this is persuasion. The title says it plainly: the home is "the sphere of woman," her proper and natural place.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_img_purpose_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_img_purpose_screen -->


<!-- ============================================================
     CHAT-EMBEDDED — synthesis: evaluate peer comparisons (Part 4)
     ============================================================ -->

<Vertical id="tmb_synth_peereval_screen">
<CapaProblem id="tmb_synth_peereval_problem" title="Then and now: the best comparison" maxAttempts="1">
<KeyGrader id="tmb_synth_peereval_grader">
<ChoiceInput id="tmb_synth_peereval">
<Markdown>
Your group each summed up the biggest difference between the two — the 1800s
ideal and today's tradwife trend. Which comparison is **best**?
</Markdown>
  <Distractor value="joe_same"              > **Joe:** They're really the same thing — both are about women choosing to stay home and care for their families instead of working, whether in the 1800s or now. </Distractor>
         <Key value="danielle_duty_vs_image"> **Danielle:** Both idealize the wife at home, but then it was a moral and religious duty; today it's a social-media image that often hides economic precarity.   </Key>
  <Distractor value="karis_family_size"     > **Karis:** The biggest difference is that women in the 1800s had far more children than today's tradwives do, so the home was more work.                         </Distractor>
</ChoiceInput>
</KeyGrader>

<Markdown when="@tmb_synth_peereval.value === 'joe_same' &amp;&amp; @tmb_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Joe has the likeness right, but a comparison owes us the difference too, and the two give very different reasons for the same picture.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_synth_peereval.value === 'karis_family_size' &amp;&amp; @tmb_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Family size isn't a contrast either text draws. Compare what the ideal MEANT: a moral duty then, an image (often masking hardship) now.*
</Markdown>
<Markdown when="@tmb_synth_peereval.value === 'danielle_duty_vs_image' &amp;&amp; @tmb_synth_peereval_problem.submitCount &gt; 0">
*Yes — both idealize the woman at home; what changed is the reason. A moral and religious duty then; a social-media image, often hiding economic precarity, now. That's the comparison your final paragraph needs.*
</Markdown>
</CapaProblem> <!-- tmb_synth_peereval_problem -->
</Vertical> <!-- tmb_synth_peereval_screen -->

</Vertical> <!-- temperance_b_full_activities_part1 -->
